== The Preface

#smallcaps[There] is no question at all that is of greater importance to mankind, and none that it more concerns every individual person to have well settled, than this: #emph[What are the distinguishing qualifications of those who are in favor with God and entitled to his eternal rewards?] Or, which comes to the same thing, #emph[What is the nature of true religion? And where do the distinguishing marks of that virtue and holiness that is acceptable in the sight of God lie?] But though it is of such importance, and though we have clear and abundant light in the word of God to direct us in this matter, there is no point on which professing Christians differ more from one another. It would be endless to count up the variety of opinions on this point that divide the Christian world. They make plain the truth of our Savior’s words: “Small is the gate and narrow the way that leads to life, and only a few find it.”

The consideration of these things has long led me to give attention to this matter, with the utmost diligence and care and the most exact search and inquiry I am capable of. It is a subject on which my mind has been especially intent ever since I first began the study of divinity. But as to the success of my inquiries, that must be left to the judgment of the reader of the following treatise.

I feel keenly that it is much more difficult to judge impartially of the subject of this discourse in the middle of the dust and smoke of such a state of controversy about things of this nature as this land is now in. As it is more difficult to write impartially, so it is more difficult to read impartially. Many will probably be hurt in their spirits to find so much that belongs to religious affection condemned here. And perhaps indignation and contempt will be stirred up in others by finding so much here justified and approved. And it may be that some will be ready to charge me with being inconsistent with myself, in approving some things so much and condemning others so much. I have found that some have always raised this objection against me, ever since the beginning of our recent controversies about religion. It is a hard thing to be a hearty, zealous friend of what has been #emph[good] and glorious in the recent extraordinary appearances, and to rejoice much in it, and at the same time to see the evil and harmful tendency of what has been #emph[bad,] and earnestly to oppose that. Yet I am #emph[humbly,] but #emph[fully,] persuaded that we will never be in the way of truth, nor go on in a way acceptable to God and tending to the advancement of Christ’s kingdom, until we do so. There is indeed something very mysterious in it, that so much good and so much bad should be mixed together in the #emph[church of God.] In the same way it is a mysterious thing, and one that has puzzled and amazed many a good Christian, that something so divine and precious as the saving grace of God, the new and divine nature, should dwell in the same heart with so much corruption, hypocrisy and iniquity in #emph[a particular saint.] Yet neither of these is more mysterious than it is real. And neither of them is a new or rare thing. It is nothing new that much false religion should prevail at a time of great reviving of true religion, and that at such a time multitudes of hypocrites should spring up among true saints. It was so in the great reformation and revival of religion in #emph[Josiah]’s time, as appears from Jeremiah 3:10 and 4:3–4, and also from the great apostasy there was in the land so soon after his reign. It was so in the great outpouring of the Spirit on the #emph[Jews] in the days of #emph[John the Baptist,] as appears from the great apostasy of that people so soon after so general an awakening, and from the temporary religious comforts and joys of many: “You#super[+] were willing for a season to rejoice in his light” (John 5:35). It was so in the great stirrings among the multitude caused by the preaching of Jesus Christ: #emph[of the many who were then called, few were chosen.] Of the multitude who were roused and moved by his preaching, and who at one time or another appeared mightily engaged, full of wonder at Christ and lifted up with joy, only a few were true disciples, who stood the shock of the great trials that came afterward and endured to the end. Many were like the stony ground or the thorny ground, and only a few, by comparison, like the good ground. Of the whole heap that was gathered, a great part was chaff, which the wind afterward drove away, and the heap of wheat that was left was small by comparison. All this appears abundantly from the history of the New Testament. It was so in the great outpouring of the Spirit in the apostles’ days, as appears from Matthew 24:10–13; Gal 3:1 and 4:11, 15; Phil 2:21 and 3:18–19; the two epistles to the #emph[Corinthians;] and many other parts of the New Testament. And so it was in the great #emph[Reformation] from popery. It plainly appears that in the visible church of God, in times of great reviving of religion, it has been again and again as it is with fruit trees in the spring. There are a multitude of blossoms, all of which look fair and beautiful, and there is a promising appearance of young fruit. But many of them last only a short time; they soon fall off and never come to maturity.

Not that we are to suppose it will always be so. In this world there will never be entire purity, either in particular saints, as a perfect freedom from mixtures of corruption, or in the church of God, without any mixture of hypocrites with saints, and of counterfeit religion and false appearances of grace with true religion and real holiness. Yet it is evident that a time will come of much greater purity in the church of God than there has been in ages past. This is plain from these texts of Scripture: Isa 52:1; Ezek 44:6, 7, 9; Joel 3:17; Zech 14:21; Ps 69:32, 35, 36; Isa 35:8, 10; Isa 4:3–4; Ezek 20:38; Ps 37:9, 10, 11, 29. And one great reason for it will be that at that time God will give his people much greater light to distinguish between true religion and its counterfeits: “And He will sit as a refiner and purifier of silver; He will purify the sons of Levi and refine them like gold and silver. Then they will present offerings to the #smallcaps[Lord] in righteousness” (Mal 3:3). With this goes verse 18, which continues the prophecy of the same happy times: “So you#super[+] will again distinguish between the righteous and the wicked, between those who serve God and those who do not.”

It is by the mixture of counterfeit religion with true, not discerned and distinguished, that the devil has had his greatest advantage against the cause and kingdom of Christ all along until now. It is plainly by this means above all that he has prevailed against every revival of religion there has ever been since the first founding of the Christian church. By this he hurt the cause of Christianity in and after the apostolic age much more than by all the persecutions of both #emph[Jews] and heathens. The apostles in all their epistles show themselves much more concerned about the first mischief than the second. By this #emph[Satan] prevailed against the Reformation begun by #emph[Luther, Zwingli] and others, to stop its progress and bring it into disgrace, ten times more than by all the bloody, cruel and previously unheard-of persecutions of the church of #emph[Rome.] By this above all he has prevailed against the revivals of religion there have been in our nation since the Reformation. By this he prevailed against #emph[New England] about a hundred years ago, to quench the love and spoil the joy of her espousals. And I think I have had opportunity enough to see plainly that by this the devil has prevailed against the recent great revival of religion in #emph[New England,] so happy and promising in its beginning. Here, most evidently, has been the main advantage #emph[Satan] has had against us. By this he has defeated us. It is by this means that the daughter of Zion in this land now lies on the ground in the pitiful condition in which we now see her: her garments torn, her face disfigured, her nakedness exposed, her limbs broken, wallowing in the blood of her own wounds and in no way able to rise. And this has come so quickly after her recent great joys and hopes. “Zion stretches out her hands, but there is no one to comfort her. The #smallcaps[Lord] has decreed against Jacob that his neighbors become his foes. Jerusalem has become an unclean thing among them” (Lam 1:17). I have seen the devil prevail in the same way against two great revivals of religion in this country. #emph[Satan] goes on with mankind as he began with them. He prevailed against our first parents, cast them out of #emph[paradise,] and suddenly brought all their happiness and glory to an end, by appearing to be a friend to their happy #emph[paradisal] state and pretending to advance it to higher degrees. In the same way, that same cunning serpent who deceived #emph[Eve] by his craftiness has, by turning us aside from the simplicity that is in Christ, suddenly succeeded in robbing us of the fair prospect we had a little while ago of a kind of paradisal state of the church of God in #emph[New England.]

After religion has revived in the church of God and enemies appear, the people who are engaged to defend its cause are commonly most exposed where they least feel any danger. While they are wholly intent on the opposition that appears openly before them, to stand against it, and neglect to look carefully all around them, the devil comes up behind them and gives a fatal stab unseen. And he has opportunity to strike a more telling blow and wound the deeper, because he strikes at his leisure and as he pleases, hindered by no guard or resistance.

And so it is likely always to be in the church, whenever religion revives remarkably, until we have learned well to distinguish between true and false religion: between saving affections and experiences, and the many fair shows and glittering appearances by which they are counterfeited. When these are not distinguished, the consequences are often unspeakably dreadful. #emph[By this means] the devil gratifies himself by bringing it about that multitudes offer to God, as though it were a pleasing and acceptable sacrifice to him, what is in fact above all things abominable to him. #emph[By this means] he deceives great multitudes about the state of their souls, making them think they are something when they are nothing, and so ruins them eternally. And not only that: he establishes many in a strong confidence of their eminent holiness who are, in God’s sight, some of the vilest of hypocrites. #emph[By this means] he damps and wounds religion in the hearts of the saints in many ways. He obscures it and deforms it by corrupt mixtures. He causes their religious affections to degenerate woefully, and sometimes, for a considerable time, to be like the #emph[manna] that bred worms and stank. He dreadfully ensnares and confounds the minds of others of the saints, brings them into great difficulties and temptations, and entangles them in a wilderness from which they can by no means free themselves. #emph[By this means Satan] mightily encourages the hearts of the open enemies of religion, strengthens their hands, fills them with weapons, and makes their fortresses strong, while at the same time religion and the church of God lie exposed to them like a city without walls. #emph[By this means] he brings it about that men work wickedness under the notion of doing God service, and so sin without restraint, indeed with eager forwardness and zeal, and with all their might. #emph[By this means] he brings even the friends of religion, without their perceiving it, to do the work of enemies, destroying religion far more effectually than open enemies can, under the notion of advancing it. #emph[By this means] the devil scatters the flock of Christ and sets them against one another, and that with great heat of spirit, under the notion of zeal for God. Religion by degrees degenerates into empty wrangling. And during the strife #emph[Satan] leads both parties far out of the right way, driving each to great extremes, one to the right hand and the other to the left, according as he finds they are most inclined, or most easily moved and swayed, until the right path in the middle is almost wholly neglected. And in the middle of this confusion the devil has great opportunity to advance his own interest and make it strong in countless ways, to get the government of all into his own hands, and to work his own will. And by what they see of the terrible consequences of this counterfeit religion, when it is not distinguished from true religion, God’s people in general have their minds unhinged and unsettled in things of religion. They do not know where to set their foot, or what to think or do. Many are brought to doubt whether there is anything at all in religion, and heresy, unbelief and atheism greatly prevail.

Therefore it greatly concerns us to use our utmost endeavors to discern clearly, and to have it well settled and established, what true religion consists in. Until this is done, we may expect that great revivals of religion will last only a short time. Until this is done, there is little good to be expected from all our heated debates, in conversation and from the press, since we do not know clearly and distinctly what we ought to contend for.

My design in the following treatise is to contribute my mite, and to use my best (however feeble) endeavors to this end. It must be noted that my design here is somewhat different from the design of what I published before. That was to show #emph[the distinguishing marks of a work of the Spirit of God,] including both his common and his saving operations. But what I aim at now is to show the nature and signs of the #emph[gracious operations] of God’s Spirit, by which they are to be distinguished from all things whatever, of which the minds of men are the subjects, that are not of a saving nature. If I have succeeded in this aim in any tolerable measure, I hope it will tend to promote the interest of religion. And whether or not I have succeeded in bringing any light to this subject, and however my attempt may be reproached in these faultfinding, censorious times, I hope in the mercy of a gracious and righteous God for the acceptance of the sincerity of my endeavors. I hope also for the fairness and the prayers of the true followers of the meek and charitable Lamb of God.
